# CraftCanvas Architecture Remediation

This walkthrough documents the technical debt reduction and infrastructure improvements made to the CraftCanvas application based on the architectural evaluation.

## Changes Made

### 1. Priority Bug Fixes
- **Middleware Scoping**: Moved the Request-ID middleware outside of the [global_exception_handler](file:///Users/oli/Desktop/CraftCanvas/backend/main.py#60-78) function in [main.py](file:///Users/oli/Desktop/CraftCanvas/backend/main.py) so that it actually executes on every request.
- **Undefined Variables**: Fixed a bug in `get_courses` where a `statement` variable was referenced before assignment, replacing it with a correct `SQLModel` select query.
- **Orphaned Jobs**: Registered the [calendar_sync_job](file:///Users/oli/Desktop/CraftCanvas/backend/jobs/scheduler.py#75-118) in [scheduler.py](file:///Users/oli/Desktop/CraftCanvas/backend/jobs/scheduler.py) so that calendar syncing actually occurs as designed.

### 2. Deconstructing the [ai_tools.py](file:///Users/oli/Desktop/CraftCanvas/backend/services/ai_tools.py) Monolith
The 1,926-line [ai_tools.py](file:///Users/oli/Desktop/CraftCanvas/backend/services/ai_tools.py) file was extremely difficult to navigate. It has been successfully split into a modular package:
- [backend/services/ai_tools/courses.py](file:///Users/oli/Desktop/CraftCanvas/backend/services/ai_tools/courses.py)
- [backend/services/ai_tools/tasks.py](file:///Users/oli/Desktop/CraftCanvas/backend/services/ai_tools/tasks.py)
- [backend/services/ai_tools/materials.py](file:///Users/oli/Desktop/CraftCanvas/backend/services/ai_tools/materials.py)
- [backend/services/ai_tools/misc.py](file:///Users/oli/Desktop/CraftCanvas/backend/services/ai_tools/misc.py)
- [backend/services/ai_tools/helpers.py](file:///Users/oli/Desktop/CraftCanvas/backend/services/ai_tools/helpers.py)

The new [__init__.py](file:///Users/oli/Desktop/CraftCanvas/backend/jobs/__init__.py) file aggregates all tools into the `TOOL_DEFINITIONS` list, preserving seamless compatibility with [ai_service.py](file:///Users/oli/Desktop/CraftCanvas/backend/services/ai_service.py) without requiring changes to how tools are loaded.

### 3. Deconstructing the [page.tsx](file:///Users/oli/Desktop/CraftCanvas/frontend/app/page.tsx) Dashboard
The 730-line Next.js dashboard had too many intertwined state variables and side effects. We extracted all data fetching and state management into three dedicated custom hooks:
- **[useDashboardData](file:///Users/oli/Desktop/CraftCanvas/frontend/hooks/useDashboardData.ts#6-152)**: Manages all core data (tasks, assignments, courses, brief, triage, fluid suggestions).
- **[useDashboardTimetable](file:///Users/oli/Desktop/CraftCanvas/frontend/hooks/useDashboardTimetable.ts#5-47)**: Manages today's timetable slots and the multi-day lookahead logic.
- **[useDashboardChat](file:///Users/oli/Desktop/CraftCanvas/frontend/hooks/useDashboardChat.ts#11-127)**: Manages the SSE streaming connection, chat history, and chat UI state.

[page.tsx](file:///Users/oli/Desktop/CraftCanvas/frontend/app/page.tsx) is now significantly smaller and strictly focused on composing UI components.

### 4. Database Migrations via Alembic
We introduced Alembic to manage database schema changes safely moving forward:
- Initialized Alembic in the backend.
- Configured [alembic/env.py](file:///Users/oli/Desktop/CraftCanvas/backend/alembic/env.py) to correctly import and read `SQLModel.metadata` from all 24 database models.
- Generated the first `baseline_schema` migration, capturing the current state of the database.

### 5. DevOps & CI/CD Pipeline
To ensure long-term stability and enforce code quality, we provisioned standard DevOps infrastructure:
- **Backend Dockerfile**: A lightweight Python 3.12 slim image to serve the FastAPI application.
- **Frontend Dockerfile**: A multi-stage Node 20 Alpine image that builds and serves the Next.js application in production mode.
- **GitHub Actions**: Added [.github/workflows/ci.yml](file:///Users/oli/Desktop/CraftCanvas/.github/workflows/ci.yml) to automatically run Python limits and PyTest, and to verify the Next.js production build on every push and pull request.

## Validation Results

- ✅ **Backend Health**: The `pytest tests/test_routers/test_health.py` test suite passed.
- ✅ **Frontend Build**: `npm run build` completed successfully, proving the TypeScript refactoring of the hooks and [page.tsx](file:///Users/oli/Desktop/CraftCanvas/frontend/app/page.tsx) introduced no type violations or missing imports.
- ✅ **Import Resolution**: verified that Python can successfully load the 40+ modularized `TOOL_DEFINITIONS` from the new `ai_tools` package.
